 Dilemma, I'm trying to determine whether A-FDT cooperates or defects in light of this dependence.\n\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Analyzing A-FDT Dynamics**\n\nI'm currently dissecting the Prisoner's Dilemma scenario for two A-FDT LLMs. I'm focusing on the core equation of the theory, specifically Equation (4) from Yudkowsky & Soares. This should reveal the nuances of how the LLMs would arrive at their choices.\n\n\n**Exploring A-FDT Nuances**\n\nI've hit a snag. Re-reading the setup, the essay emphasizes independent LLM executions with differing private observations. I'm wrestling with how A-FDT's core equation handles these distinct private inputs, given its focus on expected values. How does it resolve the inherent uncertainty about the other LLM's private information, and how does this impact the decision-making process?\n\n\n**Investigating Input Symmetry**\n\nI'm now digging into input symmetry within the A-FDT framework, particularly focusing on how it handles differing observations, represented as \"$x$.\" The essay highlights the update mechanism from $x$ to $y$, but I'm questioning the implications for twin scenarios and asymmetric inputs. I'm trying to determine whether the decision rule is invariant to specific inputs or if it relies on them, specifically in Section 6.3. I'm exploring the implications for A-FDT agents with symmetric code structures.\n\n\n**Clarifying Subjunctive Dependence**\n\nI'm now clarifying the application of subjunctive dependence in this context. It seems FDT/A-FDT agents, even with private observations, treat the other agent's action as subjectively dependent on their own decision rule, because both are the exact same LLM instance. Examining the Prisoner's Dilemma, I'm trying to determine whether A-FDT cooperates or defects in light of this dependence.\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_t